#!/bin/bash
# land-budget.sh — the routine is built to a budget, and the profile folds away
# once it has done its job.
#
# BOTH RAILS IN ONE COMMIT. The API gains two routes and the web app gains the
# page that calls them; route-reach.spec refuses a route nothing asks for, and
# it is right to. Push once, and Railway and Vercel each take their half.
#
# PRECONDITION: land-the-shop.sh.
set -euo pipefail

cd "$(dirname "$0")"
[ -d together-city-react ] || { echo "!! Run this from the Together-Ai repo root."; exit 1; }

if [ -f .git/index.lock ] && [ ! -s .git/index.lock ]; then
  echo "== clearing a stale empty .git/index.lock"
  rm -f .git/index.lock
fi

NEEDS="No routine before a budget"
LOG="$(git log --oneline -40)"
case "$LOG" in
  *"$NEEDS"*) echo "== \"$NEEDS\" is already here. Nothing to do."; exit 0 ;;
esac
case "$LOG" in
  *"Seventy products, and a way through them"*) ;;
  *) echo "!! Run land-the-shop.sh first — this is written against the tree it produces."; exit 1 ;;
esac

ALLOWED='^ M together-city-react/(public/assets/img/(apple-touch-icon-180|tc-icon-1024|tc-icon-192|tc-icon-512|tc-icon-maskable-512)\.png|public/downloads/TogetherCity\.apk)$'
DIRTY="$(git status --porcelain \
  | grep -Ev '^\?\? (land-.*\.sh|push-.*\.sh|.*\.patch)$' \
  | grep -Ev "$ALLOWED" || true)"
if [ -n "$DIRTY" ]; then
  echo "!! The tree carries changes this script did not expect:"; echo "$DIRTY"; exit 1
fi

PATCH="$(mktemp "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/land.XXXXXX")"
trap 'rm -f "$PATCH"' EXIT
cat <<'B64EOF' | tr -d '\n' | openssl base64 -d -A > "$PATCH"

B64EOF
WANT="dd1993030ee7f14fd077f03b9281c74ad643c2ed0180a88a4264543096320d25"
GOT="$(shasum -a 256 "$PATCH" | awk '{print $1}')"
[ "$GOT" = "$WANT" ] || { echo "!! Patch is corrupt."; echo "   want $WANT"; echo "   got  $GOT"; exit 1; }
echo "== patch verified"
git apply --check -C1 "$PATCH" || { echo "!! Patch does not apply cleanly. Nothing written."; exit 1; }
git apply -C1 "$PATCH"
echo "== applied"

cd together-city-chat
npx tsc --noEmit
npx jest src/beauty src/security
cd ../together-city-react
npx tsc --noEmit
npx vitest run
node scripts/a11y-audit.mjs
node scripts/motion-ceiling.mjs
node scripts/lint-ceiling.mjs
npx vite build
cd ..

git add together-city-chat/src/beauty/monthly-cost.ts \
        together-city-chat/src/beauty/budget-routine.ts \
        together-city-chat/src/beauty/budget-is-a-limit.spec.ts \
        together-city-chat/src/beauty/beauty.service.ts \
        together-city-chat/src/beauty/beauty.controller.ts \
        together-city-react/src/features/beauty/components/BudgetPanel.tsx \
        together-city-react/src/features/beauty/components/Collapsible.tsx \
        together-city-react/src/features/beauty/pages/Budget.tsx \
        together-city-react/src/features/beauty/pages/Profile.tsx \
        together-city-react/src/features/beauty/pages/Routine.tsx \
        together-city-react/src/features/beauty/api.ts \
        together-city-react/src/config/hubs.ts \
        together-city-react/src/app/router.tsx
git commit -F - <<'MSG'
No routine before a budget

The hub handed out a routine and never asked what anybody could spend on it.
The step goes in between: profile, budget, routine — and the budget is a hard
monthly limit per category, not an estimate printed next to a finished answer.

THE ORDER OF OPERATIONS IS THE WHOLE FEATURE. The obvious implementation builds
the ideal routine, prices it, and tells you it costs more than you have. That is
a shop wearing an advisor's coat. This works the other way: understand the need,
establish the minimum that actually treats it, apply the budget, choose within
it. The number is an input to selection and never a filter over a result.

WHAT A PRODUCT COSTS PER MONTH, because comparing a budget against purchase
prices compares two different things. A ₹3,200 cleanser that lasts four months
and a ₹800 serum that lasts three weeks look like a ₹4,000 problem and are
nothing of the sort. monthly-cost.ts reads the pack size off the product's own
name, divides by an honest monthly dose, and caps the answer at twelve months
because nothing survives longer once it is open.

  THE DOSES ARE GENEROUS RATHER THAN FLATTERING. Two finger-lengths of sunscreen
  a day is 36 ml a month, so a 50 ml tube is six weeks and ₹342 a month — which
  is the number people find surprising and the reason the routine works.
  Estimating usage at what somebody SHOULD apply, rather than at what makes the
  budget look comfortable, is the difference between a plan and a sales pitch.

  Rounded UP to the rupee, because eight steps each rounded down is a routine
  that fits on the page and not in the month.

AND IT SAYS HOW LONG THINGS LAST. "₹1,099 ≈ ₹366/month" invites the question
"says who?"; "one 88 ml bottle, about three months" answers it before it is
asked. Weeks under two months, because "1.4 months" is not a thing anybody says
and rounding it to "1 month" is the difference between one sunscreen a month and
running out halfway through.

THREE BUDGETS, PLANNED INDEPENDENTLY. Face, hair and body each have their own
limit, and a generous face budget must not buy a better shampoo. A product is
charged to its own GROUP rather than to the band it lands in — a face mask and a
shampoo share the weekly band and nothing else.

LEAN IS THE DEFAULT AND NOT THE FALLBACK. Roles are one-per-routine by
construction, which is what prevents cleanser-toner-essence-serum-serum-serum
without a rule anywhere capping the count. A step enters because something in
the assessment asks for it, and `leftOut` carries the sentence: "You don't need
a separate prep step — what you already have covers it."

FOUR PASSES, AND THE ORDER IS THE POLICY. The floor (cheapest product for every
essential), then the step that treats what was actually found, then upgrades,
then optional steps — and only where they answer a need nothing else covers.

  AN UPGRADE HAS TO BUY SOMETHING MEASURABLE, and the first version had this
  wrong. Spending more on a higher MATCH SCORE produced no upgrades at all,
  because two moisturisers for the same skin type score identically and should.
  The measure is how many of this person's needs the product answers: the ₹912
  moisturiser replaces the ₹572 one because it covers one more thing in the
  assessment, and a dearer product covering exactly the same ground is refused.
  That single rule is the difference between optimising and maximising.

  A SHORT BUDGET DROPS THE MOST SUBSTITUTABLE STEP, not the last one declared.
  At ₹1,000 the face routine keeps the sunscreen and the moisturiser and loses
  the cleanser, because sunscreen is the one step with no substitute and washing
  with water is at least possible. It reports ₹1,067 as what the full floor
  would cost — a number, so the choice is real — rather than "insufficient".

  AND IT DOES NOT SPEND WHAT IS LEFT. ₹10,000, ₹25,000 and ₹60,000 all buy the
  same ₹3,911 routine on the same profile, because there is nothing left worth
  adding. The remainder is reported, not absorbed.

budget-is-a-limit.spec.ts plans at all six budgets the owner named and asserts
the routine never exceeds the cap, genuinely changes between them, holds one
product per role at every level, and that ₹60,000 leaves more than ₹40,000
unspent. Twenty-seven tests, and three of them exist because the derivation was
wrong first.

THE BUDGET LIVES IN THE PROFILE'S OWN JSON rather than in three new columns: the
whole beauty profile is already one `extras` blob on that row, and a budget is a
profile answer. `null` means NOT SET, and the difference from zero is the
feature — the routine page shows a gate, and nothing is generated behind it. A
default applied on somebody's behalf is the one thing this design refuses.

THE PROFILE FOLDS AWAY ONCE IT HAS DONE ITS JOB. It is an INPUT stage, and every
return visit used to begin with the form you already filled in, the photos you
already uploaded and the assessment you already read, between you and the
routine they exist to produce. Photos, assessment and timeline collapse;
nothing is deleted and nothing is re-asked. Two things stay open, both because
they are what you came back for: the progress photographs, and the budget.

THE BUDGET PANEL IS IN TWO PLACES AND IS ONE COMPONENT — its own page at
/beauty/budget for somebody returning to change a number, and inline directly
under the assessment, because that is step two and the assessment is what the
money is being spent against. A second copy would be a second set of rounding
rules.

A slider AND a typed field, both the same value: the slider is for the shape of
the decision, since ₹1,000 is nothing like ₹40,000 and the thumb shows you that,
and the field is for people who already know their number. The chips are
shortcuts, never the only options. The slider steps in hundreds because a budget
of ₹4,973 is a value you cannot repeat; the field still takes any rupee.

The sidebar is six items now, with Budget at 02.

tsc clean both sides. Backend: 92 beauty tests across 8 files, and
route-reach.spec green — which is what forced these two rails into one commit,
since it refuses an API route the web app never calls, and it was right.
Frontend: 60 test files, a11y 0 at the ceiling, motion and lint at theirs, vite
build clean. The budget page rendered at 760 before it was called done.

MSG
echo "== committed"

echo
echo "==============================================================="
echo " Landed: $NEEDS"
echo " Profile → Budget → Routine. Face, hair and body each capped,"
echo " and the engine chooses within them. Push: Railway and Vercel"
echo " each take their half."
echo "==============================================================="
